{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775376488-6BqWU5HugXqxFADYSD7Y","role":"assistant","created":1775376505,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the data I need. Let me extract the January New Aa corporate bond yields for each year from 1990 to 1999:\n\nFrom the data:\n- 1990 Jan: 9.56\n- 1991 Jan: 9.60\n- 1992 Jan: 8.36\n- 1993 Jan: 7.91\n- 1994 Jan: 7.16\n- 1995 Jan: 8.59\n- 1996 Jan: 7.00\n- 1997 Jan: 7.62\n- 1998 Jan: 6.62\n- 1999 Jan: 6.14\n\nNow I need to calculate the expected shortfall at 95% confidence using the historical portfolio return approach.\n\nExpected Shortfall (ES) at 95% confidence is also known as Conditional Value at Risk (CVaR). It represents the expected return in the worst 5% of cases.\n\nFirst, I need to convert yields to returns. Since these are yields, I need to calculate the periodic returns. For bond yields, one common approach is to use the yield change or to treat the yield as the return. However, for the \"historical portfolio return approach\" with yields, we typically:\n\n1. Use the yields directly as returns, or\n2. Calculate returns from yields (which is more complex)\n\nGiven the context (these are yield percentages), I'll interpret them as the returns for each period. The question asks for expected shortfall using \"historical portfolio return approach\" on the \"reported yield percentage values\".\n\nSo I'll treat these yield values as the returns:\n- 1990 Jan: 9.56%\n- 1991 Jan: 9.60%\n- 1992 Jan: 8.36%\n- 1993 Jan: 7.91%\n- 1994 Jan: 7.16%\n- 1995 Jan: 8.59%\n\n\n- 1996 Jan: 7.00%\n- 1997 Jan: 7.62%\n- 1998 Jan: 6.62%\n- 1999 Jan: 6.14%\n\nI'll proceed with calculating the returns in decimal form by dividing the percentages by 100. This will help me determine the 5th percentile for the expected shortfall calculation.\n\nOrdering the returns from smallest to largest reveals the distribution. I need to identify the bottom 5% of returns, which means finding the value at the 0.05 quantile with 10 data points.\n\nWith 10 data points, the exact position falls between two values. I'll interpolate between the first and second smallest returns to get a precise 5th percentile estimate. The calculation involves linear interpolation to find the value that represents the lowest 5% of returns.\n\nThe expected shortfall requires averaging all returns below this 5th percentile threshold. However, the small sample size of only 10 data points creates uncertainty about how to handle the boundary conditions. I'm carefully considering whether to include or exclude the boundary value in this calculation.\n\nI'll compute the expected shortfall by identifying the worst 5% of returns, which with 10 observations means selecting the lowest return. The 5th percentile corresponds to the minimum value in this small dataset. I'll interpolate between the first two sorted returns to determine the precise threshold.\n\nThe quantile calculation involves finding the value where 5% of observations fall below, using linear interpolation between the sorted returns. This approach ensures a precise representation of the extreme tail risk.\n\nI'll use Python to perform the precise numerical calculation, which will help me determine the expected shortfall more accurately.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_a8b6edc036354c0094d4f06b","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF'\nimport numpy as np\n\n# January New Aa corporate bond yields for 1990-1999\nyields = [9.56, 9.60, 8.36, 7.91, 7.16, 8.59, 7.00, 7.62, 6.62, 6.14]\n\n# Sort the yields\nsorted_yields = sorted(yields)\nprint(\"Sorted yields:\", sorted_yields)\n\n# For expected shortfall at 95% confidence, we need the average of the worst 5% of returns\n# With 10 observations, 5% = 0.5 observations, so we take the worst return(s)\n# At 95% confidence, we look at the 5th percentile and average all returns below it\n\n# Using the historical method with linear interpolation for the percentile\nn = len(yields)\nalpha = 0.05  # 95% confidence means we look at the 5% tail\n\n# Calculate the index for the percentile\nindex = (n - 1) * alpha\nlower_idx = int(np.floor(index))\nupper_idx = int(np.ceil(index))\n\nprint(f\"Index: {index}, Lower: {lower_idx}, Upper: {upper_idx}\")\n\nif lower_idx == upper_idx:\n    percentile_5 = sorted_yields[lower_idx]\nelse:\n    percentile_5 = sorted_yields[lower_idx] + (index - lower_idx) * (sorted_yields[upper_idx] - sorted_yields[lower_idx])\n\nprint(f\"5th percentile: {percentile_5}\")\n\n# Expected shortfall is the average of all returns below the 5th percentile\n# Since we have very few observations in the tail, we'll include all returns at or below the percentile\nreturns_below = [y for y in yields if y <= percentile_5]\nprint(f\"Returns at or below 5th percentile: {returns_below}\")\n\nexpected_shortfall = np.mean(returns_below)\nprint(f\"Expected Shortfall at 95% confidence: {expected_shortfall}\")\nprint(f\"Rounded to nearest hundredth: {round(expected_shortfall, 2)}\")\nEOF"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
