{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775376542-hduA6BE9Duu7kPX0eEG0","role":"assistant","created":1775376565,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me reconsider this. The question asks about \"expected shortfall at 95% confidence using the historical portfolio return approach\" for yield values. \n\nThe issue is that yields are rates, not returns. However, when looking at bond performance, higher yields generally mean lower prices (negative returns), and lower yields mean higher prices (positive returns).\n\nBut the question specifically says \"for the reported yield percentage values\" - so we're looking at the yields themselves, not converting them to returns.\n\nIn the historical VaR/ES approach:\n1. We have historical observations (yields in this case)\n2. For 95% confidence, we look at the 5th percentile (the worst 5% of cases)\n3. Expected Shortfall is the average of all values in the worst 5%\n\nWith 10 observations:\n- 5% of 10 = 0.5 observations\n- This is typically rounded up to 1 observation\n\nSo the Expected Shortfall would be the lowest yield, which is 6.14%.\n\nHowever, I'm realizing there might be a different interpretation. In finance, when calculating VaR/ES, we often look at losses. If these are yields, perhaps we should think about \"negative returns\" meaning higher yields = worse outcome.\n\nActually, wait - let me re-read the question. It asks about \"expected shortfall at 95% confidence using the historical portfolio return approach.\" \n\nWhen dealing with yields vs returns:\n- Higher bond yields mean lower prices (capital loss)\n- Lower bond yields mean higher prices (capital gain)\n\nSo if we're looking at \"returns\" approach with bond yields, the \"worst\" outcomes would be the highest yields (biggest capital losses), not the lowest yields.\n\nLet me reconsider with this interpretation - treating higher yields as negative returns (losses).\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_dd012aef41394655b4eebff8","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF'\n# Let me reconsider the problem from a different angle.\n# In bond investing, higher yields mean lower prices (capital losses)\n# So when calculating risk/shortfall, we should consider HIGH yields as negative outcomes\n\n# For bond yields:\n# - Higher yields = lower prices = capital loss (worse outcome)\n# - Lower yields = higher prices = capital gain (better outcome)\n\n# So when calculating expected shortfall, we should look at the HIGHEST yields\n# (representing the worst outcomes in terms of returns)\n\nyields = [9.56, 9.60, 8.36, 7.91, 7.16, 8.59, 7.00, 7.62, 6.62, 6.14]\nsorted_yields = sorted(yields, reverse=True)  # Sort descending (highest first)\nprint(\"Sorted yields (highest first):\", sorted_yields)\n\n# For 95% confidence, we want the average of the worst 5% of returns\n# In bond terms, worst returns = highest yields\n# 5% of 10 observations = 0.5, round up to 1\n\nn = len(yields)\nbottom_5_pct = max(1, round(0.05 * n))\nprint(f\"Number of observations in worst 5%: {bottom_5_pct}\")\n\n# The worst outcomes (highest yields)\nworst_yields = sorted_yields[:bottom_5_pct]\nprint(f\"Worst yields (highest): {worst_yields}\")\n\nexpected_shortfall = sum(worst_yields) / len(worst_yields)\nprint(f\"\\nExpected Shortfall at 95%: {expected_shortfall}\")\nprint(f\"Rounded to nearest hundredth: {round(expected_shortfall, 2)}\")\n\n# But let's also check the more formal approach with interpolation\n# For 5th percentile of a right-tailed distribution (high yields = bad)\n# Actually, let's recalculate this properly\n\n# With 10 observations, sorted descending:\n# The 5th percentile in the upper tail would be at index = 0.05 * (n+1) from the end\n# Or more simply, we find the value that 5% of observations exceed\n\n# Method: For expected shortfall at 95% confidence (left tail)\n# We find the 5th percentile and average everything below it\n# If \"worst\" = highest yields, then we look at the TOP 5% of yields\n\nprint(\"\\n--- Using formal expected shortfall calculation ---\")\n# Sort ascending and find the 95th percentile (since we want worst 5% = top 5% of yields)\nsorted_asc = sorted(yields)\nn = len(yields)\n\n# For expected shortfall at 95%, we want the average of all values \n# in the top 5% (worst 5% of returns for bonds = highest yields)\n# The cutoff is at the 95th percentile\n\n# Index for 95th percentile\nindex_95 = 0.95 * (n + 1)\nprint(f\"95th percentile index: {index_95}\")\n\n# Get the 95th percentile value\nimport math\nlower_idx = int(math.floor(index_95)) - 1\nupper_idx = int(math.ceil(index_95)) - 1\nif lower_idx < 0:\n    lower_idx = 0\nif upper_idx >= n:\n    upper_idx = n - 1\n    \nfraction = index_95 - int(index_95)\npercentile_95 = sorted_asc[lower_idx] * (1 - fraction) + sorted_asc[upper_idx] * fraction\nprint(f\"95th percentile value: {percentile_95}\")\n\n# Expected shortfall = average of all values >= 95th percentile\nes_values = [y for y in yields if y >= percentile_95]\nprint(f\"Values at or above 95th percentile: {es_values}\")\n\nes = sum(es_values) / len(es_values)\nprint(f\"Expected Shortfall: {es}\")\nprint(f\"Rounded: {round(es, 2)}\")\nEOF"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
