ted yield percentage values\", so we're dealing with yields, not prices or returns.\n\n\nIn risk management for fixed income, I need to consider what \"worst\" means when examining yield data. Since prices and yields move inversely, the highest yields represent the greatest capital losses. Using the historical method with 10 observations, the worst 5% would be 1 observation, and 9.60 represents the maximum yield in the dataset. This suggests the expected shortfall should reflect that worst-case yield scenario.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_a3f9c72137cb4a4a9745a741","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"/app/answer.txt","before":"6.14","after":"9.60"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
